finding: HEAD daa1551: two findings against the public ECS declaration. 1. src/engine/ecs/world.ts:635-651 (also src/engine/ecs/component.ts:562-567): presentation policy is latched when storage/membership metadata is first allocated, but a same-key reloaded declaration with changed presentation metadata is silently accepted. Independent reproduction: declare C={x:f32}, register/add x=1; redeclare the same key and fields with {presentation:true}, register it; take a NEW snapshot, set x=.25, restore. x becomes 1, contrary to the now-registered presentation declaration. Registry revision invalidation does not help because this snapshot was captured after registration. The reverse transition silently excludes newly simulation-declared fields from recovery. Reject recovery-policy changes for an existing stable identity, or reconcile the policy consistently; same-key aliases must not silently give a different contract from the declaration. Neither new test covers this. 2. src/engine/ecs/component.ts:366-369: the public option documents the mechanism but never restricts the state it may hold. It must explicitly rule out authoritative/simulation state and anything whose current value or membership influences replayed simulation. Setting this on health/input/authority data currently works and silently makes recovery incorrect; 'presentation' alone is not the missing contract. Describe entity-reference lifetime too: keeping the holder alive does not keep its referenced target alive across restore; reference values remain current, are not remapped, and must be resolved against restored identities. CONTRIBUTING's simulation/recovery rule is not a substitute for the declaration's public JSDoc. Independently read unit stages 5-7, CONTRIBUTING Runtime/Code/Tests, draft prediction consumer, changed ECS/storage/entity/registry paths and VisualCorrection table path. Component-local metadata is a small, defensible seam alongside Plugin.recovery (which owns hidden state), and 'presentation' fits the existing simulation/presentation split. Lightyear's opt-in predicted-component rollback is related precedent, not the same selection policy; Shallot already snapshots simulation components by default. No need to reverse that default to fix these findings. Checks: all cheap engine ECS tests: 120 pass; requested new tests, frame/snapshot recovery, visual-correction GPU tests and package check: 17 pass (package 13,770,146 bytes exactly at ceiling). Independent temporary GPU probe verified dirty .25 uploads after restore, correction-table membership disappears for a reused identity, and registered reinsertion reapplies defaults. Independent reload probe reproduced finding 1. Temporary probes removed; tree clean. GPU adapter had no identity, so no hardware qualification claimed. Test sensitivity: the new value/decay assertions distinguish the original 1-versus-.25 regression; membership/query/observer assertions cover survival, removal, addition, absent/reused/future identities. They do not exercise same-key policy changes, presentation entity references, or GPU table restoration/change marks. The decay witness uses a CPU app and synthetic draw decay, not the draft multiplayer consumer or a shipped smoothing system; appropriate stage-5 scope, not evidence for stages 6-7. The independent GPU probe covers the table/dirty-mark concern but is not committed regression coverage.
